<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.11.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01934</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.11.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>ef6ca108-7b91-4fa6-9e4a-ca8eba0d4f9d</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="e072991e-93f6-428a-b602-fd265b8bcb16" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="2" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--1-->
  <exercise data-content-id="92d59337-3613-48cb-ba17-d11f71cb511e" data-problem-type="multiselect" data-solution="[&quot;Its \\(x\\)-intercepts are at \\((0,0)\\) and \\((11,0)\\).&quot;, &quot;The \\(x\\)-coordinate of its vertex is 5.5.&quot;]" data-solution-options="[&quot;Its \\(x\\)-intercepts are at \\((-2,0)\\) and \\((11,0)\\).&quot;, &quot;Its \\(x\\)-intercepts are at \\((0,0)\\) and \\((11,0)\\).&quot;, &quot;Its \\(x\\)-intercepts are at \\((2,0)\\) and \\((-11,0)\\).&quot;, &quot;It has only one \\(x\\)-intercept.&quot;, &quot;The \\(x\\)-coordinate of its vertex is -4.5.&quot;, &quot;The \\(x\\)-coordinate of its vertex is 11.&quot;, &quot;The \\(x\\)-coordinate of its vertex is 4.5.&quot;, &quot;The \\(x\\)-coordinate of its vertex is 5.5.&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para><emphasis>Select two </emphasis>true statements about the graph that represents \(y=2x(x-11)\).</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! Its \(x\)-intercepts are at (0,0) and (11,0).The \(x\)-coordinate of its vertex is 5.5.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.11.2 and 7.11.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--2-->
  <exercise data-content-id="e72a5230-87d9-4225-b0fc-194d6408af7d" data-problem-type="multiselect" data-solution="[&quot;\\(y=(x-1)(x-3)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\( y=x(x-4)\\)&quot;]" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(y=(x-2)(x-4)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(y=(x-2)(x+2)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(y=(x-1)(x-3)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(y=x(x+4)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\( y=x(x-4)\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para><emphasis>Select two</emphasis> equations whose graphs have a vertex with \(x\)-coordinate 2.</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \(y=(x-1)(x-3)\) and \( y=x(x-4)\) </para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.11.2 and 7.11.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--3-->
  <exercise data-content-id="1e77c74e-9e17-4703-b6f8-9cb42b073a3c" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\((0,0)\) and \((2, 0)\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\((0,0)\\) and \\((0, 2)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((0,0)\\) and \\((2, 0)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((0,0)\\) and \\((0,-2)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((0,0)\\) and \\((-2,0)\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Three different equations are given. Refer to these equations to complete questions a–f.</para>
      <table class="os-raise-wideequaltable">
        <thead>
          <row>
            <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Equation</entry>
            <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">\(x\)-intercept</entry>
            <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">\(x\)-coordinate of the vertex</entry>
          </row>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <para>\(y=x(x-2)\)</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>a. _____</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>b. _____</para>
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <para>\(y=(x-4)(x+5)\)</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>c. _____</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>d. _____</para>
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <para>\(y=-5x(3-x)\)</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>e. _____</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>f. _____</para>
            </entry>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      
      <para> What are the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph that represents \(y=x(x-2)\)?</para>
        </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \((0,0)\) and \((2,0)\)</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.11.2 and 7.11.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--3b-->
  <exercise data-content-id="66b54e19-d36a-4965-a9d9-1e3943173cdb" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="1">
    <problem>
      <para> What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph that represents \(y=x(x-2)\)?</para>
      <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! 1</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para> Try again. Revisit activities 7.11.2 and 7.11.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--3c-->
  <exercise data-content-id="37652284-a5ba-426c-839a-e975a25748c9" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\((-5,0)\) and \((4,0)\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\((-5,0)\\) and \\((4,0)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((0,-5)\\) and \\((0,4)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((5,0)\\) and \\((-4,0)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((0,5)\\) and \\((0,-4)\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para> What are the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph that represents \(y=(x-4)(x+5)\)?</para>
        </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \((-5,0)\) and \((4,0)\)</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.11.2 and 7.11.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--3d-->
  <exercise data-content-id="4cac9766-6f18-4bcb-9ab9-7099ea97c8cf" data-problem-comparator="float" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="-0.5">
    <problem>
      <para> What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph that represents \(y=(x-4)(x+5)\)? </para>
        <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! -0.5</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.11.2 and 7.11.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--3e-->
  <exercise data-content-id="ee4f8051-bec4-4711-b7e4-0d39d4a1875d" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\((0,0)\) and \((3,0)\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\((0,0)\\) and \\((0,-3)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((0,0)\\) and \\((-3,0)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((0,0)\\) and \\((3,0)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((0,0)\\) and \\((0,3)\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para> What are the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph that represents \(y=-5x(3-x)\)?</para>
        </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \((0,0)\) and \((3,0)\)</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.11.2 and 7.11.3. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--3f-->
  <exercise data-content-id="f5018e25-e97d-48c6-b4e2-1557f6575971" data-problem-comparator="float" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="1.5">
    <problem>
      <para> What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph that represents \(y=-5x(3-x)\)? </para>
        <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! 1.5</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.11.2 and 7.11.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--3g-->
  <exercise data-content-id="ddcb53cd-215f-4142-a282-3ad0c570fa80" data-problem-comparator="float" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="-0.5">
    <problem>
      <para>What is the equation of the axis of symmetry of \(y = (x - 4)(x + 5)\)? (If needed, enter fractional values as a decimal.)</para>
        <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! -0.5</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.11.2 and 7.11.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q4a-->

  <exercise data-content-id="693ff91a-6e94-4ba9-b34f-7bd5d58db355" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\((2,0)\) and \((4,0)\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\((2,0)\\) and \\((4,0)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((0,2)\\) and \\((0,4)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((-2,0)\\) and \\((-4,0)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((0,-2)\\) and \\((0,-4)\\)&quot;]">

    <problem>
      <para>Answer questions a–c about the function \(y=(x-2)(x-4)\).</para>
      <para>What are the \(x\)-intercepts of the vertex of the graph \(y=(x-2)(x-4)\).</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \((2,0)\) and \((4,0)\)</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.11.2 and 7.11.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--4b-->
  <exercise data-content-id="8376bb58-0f6a-4f6c-8301-ebf69e1887f9" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="No" data-solution-options="[&quot;Yes&quot;, &quot;No&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para> Is \((-1,3)\) a point on the graph?</para>
        </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! Substituting -1 for \(x\) gives \((-1-2)(-1-4)\) or -3 -5, which is 15, so \((-1,15)\) would be a point on the graph, not \((-1,3)\).</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.11.2 and 7.11.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--4c-->
  <exercise data-content-id="70f17ceb-fe72-4533-98be-ce8ace7c8884" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="No" data-solution-options="[&quot;Yes&quot;, &quot;No&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para> Does the graph represent the equation \(y=(x-2)(x-4)\)? 
            
            <figure><media alt="Graph of nonlinear function, origin O. Horizontal axis from negative 8 to 6, by 2’s. Vertical axis from negative 8 to 12, by 4’s. Line passes through negative 7 comma 8, negative 5 comma 0, negative 3 comma 0 and 1 comma 7."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/4d7ede3e12cc95715ca6ed8c75079c4081888c1a"/></media></figure></para>
        </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.11.4.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Do not edit below this line-->
  <solution> </solution>
  <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response"> </div>
</div></section>
</content>
